"""Concise explanatory Q&A. Each question develops a distinct point; no fixed quota."""
BLOCKS=r'''
# 1
How does this report place revelation and migration within the Prophet's life?|Revelation began when he was forty. He then spent thirteen years in Makkah, migrated by command, and lived ten years in Madinah. The explanation gives his age at death as sixty-three.|E:age of sixty-three
# 2
Was al-Muddaththir the beginning of all revelation, or the renewal after its pause?|This account concerns revelation after a pause. The publisher places the initial command to read before that pause and the opening of al-Muddaththir after it. The angel was the same one who had previously come at Hira. Keeping that sequence clear prevents confusing the two occasions.|B1:ceased for a short period
What responsibilities accompany the command to arise and warn?|The explanation joins calling people to the message with proclaiming Allah's greatness, purifying garments, and abandoning idol worship. The passage moves from being covered after fear to carrying the responsibility of proclamation. It describes duties, not merely an emotional experience.|E:call people to the message
# 3
What do the two forms of revelation show about receiving and retaining it?|The Prophet describes a bell-like experience and the angel appearing as a man and speaking. The first was harder, yet he retained what had been conveyed; in the second, he understood the angel's speech. The account distinguishes the manner of reception from the certainty of retaining the message.|T:I remember what it has said
Why does Aishah mention sweat on a very cold day?|The contrast makes the physical burden of revelation clear: the sweating was observed despite the cold. Her eyewitness detail complements the Prophet's own description of the difficult experience. The source relates the bodily effect to receiving revelation, not to uncertainty about its wording.|T:forehead was dripping
# 4
How does the explanation connect the Prophet's visible distress with his response to revelation?|Ubadah observed distress and a change in facial color. The explanation relates these effects to the gravity of receiving revelation and to the Prophet's attentive reverence for Allah's commands and reports. The point is the weight of the responsibility.|E:gravity and difficulty
# 5
Why was the Prophet instructed to listen before reciting?|He had been moving his lips from concern to retain the revelation before Jibril departed. Allah assured its gathering in his heart and its recitation. He thereafter listened while Jibril recited and then recited as he had been taught. The account brings together attentive reception, divine assurance, and faithful recitation.|E:listen attentively
What is the significance of Ibn Abbas and Said demonstrating the lip movement?|They did not merely describe an action in general terms; each demonstrated the movement received from the earlier transmitter. That detail shows how an observed action was conveyed. It does not turn the earlier hastening into an instruction for learners to recite over a teacher.|T:Sa‘īd
# 6
How did the basmalah identify surah boundaries, and what exception does the source note?|The revelation of In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful marked the end of the preceding surah and the beginning of another. The supplied benefit notes the exception between al-Anfal and at-Tawbah. The subject here is the recognition of surah divisions.|B1:al-Anfāl
# 7
Why is the Quran described as an enduring prophetic sign?|The explanation presents it as both revelation and a lasting proof of the mission. Its guidance and benefit extend beyond the first witnesses to later generations. This gives the report a wider focus than the number of miracles witnessed at one moment or in one place.|E:those who will exist
Does emphasizing revelation deny that the Prophet had other miracles?|No. The quoted clarification by Ibn Hajar expressly rejects that reading. The Quran is emphasized as his greatest and distinctive miracle, while other miracles are not denied. The report also expresses his hope to have the greatest number of followers on the Day of Judgment.|B4:not to confine
# 8
What is the significance of abundant revelation near the end of the Prophet's life?|Anas reports especially abundant revelation in that final period. The explanation connects it with completing the Shariah. The account therefore directs attention to the continuing reception of guidance near the end of the mission.|E:complete the Shariah
# 9
What does refusal of Paradise mean in this exchange?|The Companions were puzzled that anyone would refuse Paradise. The Prophet explained the expression through obedience and disobedience to him. The published benefits connect obedience to the Messenger with obedience to Allah. The warning makes response to his guidance central, rather than mere knowledge of his name.|T:who disobeys me
# 10
What conditions must remain attached to this report's warning?|The wording concerns hearing of the Messenger and dying without believing in the message he brought. The mention of Jews and Christians does not restrict the mission to those two communities; the source explains its universal reach. A careful explanation retains the stated conditions rather than turning the passage into unsupported verdicts about individuals.|T:who hears of me
How does the publisher distinguish those whom the call has not reached?|Its third benefit states that those who have not heard of the Prophet and have not been reached by the call are excused, with their case in the Hereafter left to Allah. This qualification should remain visible when explaining the warning about those who hear and do not believe.|B3:left to Allah
# 11
What guidance does the report give for facing religious disagreement?|The advice begins with consciousness of Allah and directs firm adherence to the Prophet's Sunnah and that of the rightly guided caliphs. The explanation names Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and Ali. Its response to discord is adherence to received guidance, not choosing a position merely because it is new or popular.|T:Rightly-Guided Caliphs
How is obedience to a leader qualified in the source benefits?|The advice rejects making a leader's social status the basis for refusing obedience. The supplied benefit also states the limit: obedience must not involve disobeying Allah. Both points matter; neither contempt for status nor an unrestricted demand for obedience reflects the complete explanation.|B5:without disobeying Allah
What are the new matters warned against here?|The wording concerns newly introduced matters in religion. It is a warning about religious innovation and departure from the given guidance. It should not be detached from that subject and applied simply to every new tool, teaching format, or worldly arrangement.|T:new things (in religion)
# 12
Why does this report reject using the Quran as a reason to dismiss the Sunnah?|The listener claims that only what he finds in the Quran can determine lawful and unlawful. The Prophet responds by affirming the religious authority of his prohibitions. The explanation grounds this in his conveying from Allah. The central issue is rejection of authentic Prophetic authority, not the use of a couch or a sincere request to verify a report.|E:conveys from his Lord
# 13
What standard of acceptance is expressed by the two wordings of this hadith?|The first wording rejects introducing into religion what does not belong to it. The second also covers performing an act that does not conform to it, even if someone else introduced it. The explanation grounds religious practice in evidence from the Quran and Sunnah; good intention does not remove the need for conformity.|T:Whoever does something
How should this criterion be distinguished from opposition to every new development?|The source distinguishes religious additions from worldly affairs. A new means of teaching or preserving knowledge is not rejected merely because it is new. Likewise, Hadith 37 concerns implementing an established good through charity; its explanation does not authorize inventing unsupported rituals.|B6:worldly affairs
# 14
How does the hadith relate obedience to a person's ability?|It directs avoidance of prohibitions and performance of commands to the extent one is able. Capacity is therefore relevant to carrying out a command, without making willingness to obey optional. The instruction holds responsibility and real human limitations together.|T:as much as you can
Does the warning against excessive questioning discourage necessary learning?|The benefits expressly distinguish questions needed to learn the religion from questioning driven by obstinacy or artificial difficulty. The warning concerns destructive questioning and disagreement with the prophets. A sincere question about understanding or carrying out a duty belongs to the approved kind of learning.|B3:aims at learning
# 15
Why were the proposed commitments corrected despite the Companions' desire for worship?|They reasoned that they needed greater severity because the Prophet's sins had been forgiven. He corrected the idea of seeking greater perfection outside his example: he prayed and slept, fasted and broke the fast, and married. Their good aim did not make an excessive method the better path.|E:sees perfection in something else
What kind of balance does the source recommend?|Its benefits recommend maintaining worship, including obligatory and voluntary acts, while fulfilling others' rights. Moderation is not idleness, and devotion is not measured by abandoning every lawful need. The Prophet's household life and worship provide the model discussed in this account.|B8:rights of others
# 16
Why did manageable deeds not indicate a lower level of devotion?|The Companions asked for harder deeds, reasoning from the Prophet's forgiven status. He answered that he knew Allah best and feared Him most. The explanation connects manageable commands with consistency. Knowing Allah and revering Him therefore support following this guidance, rather than treating severity itself as the measure of devotion.|E:maintain it consistently
# 17
What is meant by understanding the religion as a sign of good?|The report identifies understanding the religion as a gift from Allah. The benefits consequently urge seeking and learning religious knowledge. Its focus is understanding the guidance, rather than equating religious good with collecting information, public recognition, or success in argument.|T:understanding of the religion
Why does the Prophet distinguish distributing from giving?|He describes himself as a distributor and Allah as the Giver. The explanation includes sustenance and knowledge among what Allah granted him to distribute. The distinction recognizes the means through which a benefit arrives while affirming its dependence on Allah.|E:sustenance, knowledge
What continuity does the closing assurance concern?|It concerns continued adherence to Allah's laws within the Ummah despite opposition. The benefits explain that if one group forsakes the truth, another will follow it. This is not a statement that every member will always agree or that believers will experience no worldly difficulty.|B2:another will follow
# 18
What distinguishes the three responses represented by the land?|Fertile ground represents understanding, practicing, and teaching guidance. Water-retaining ground represents preserving knowledge from which others benefit, even without the same depth of understanding. Barren ground represents neither retaining the guidance nor benefiting oneself or others through it. The same rain produces different results because its recipients respond differently.|E:three types
Why is preserving knowledge still valuable when another person understands it more deeply?|The second type of ground does not grow vegetation but keeps water available for others. The explanation uses this to show the benefit of preserving and transmitting knowledge without claiming the highest level of deduction. The parable values that contribution while also showing the fuller benefit of understanding and practice.|E:holds water
# 19
Why can the recipient of a hadith understand more than its first hearer?|Faithful preservation and deep comprehension are related but distinct abilities. The transmitter may convey accurately, while a recipient has greater insight or ability to derive rulings. The report honors reliable transmission without making the first hearer automatically the strongest interpreter.|E:deeper insight
How does the source understand preservation for faithful transmission?|The benefits include both preservation in memory and preservation in writing. These serve conveying the teaching accurately. The distinction also explains why a person's strength in transmission need not mean that they can independently derive every ruling contained in a report.|B7:two types
# 20
How does conveying even a little remain compatible with responsible teaching?|The explanation permits passing on a small amount, provided the person knows what they convey. The closing warning against deliberate false attribution makes accuracy essential. Limited knowledge is not a reason to invent missing material, but neither must a learner know everything before responsibly sharing something verified.|E:knowledge of what he conveys
What limit applies to narrating accounts of the Children of Israel?|The explanation permits such narration when it does not conflict with the Shariah. Permission to mention an account is not a declaration that every earlier story is authenticated as Prophetic teaching. The distinction matters when selecting historical or illustrative material for a lesson.|E:does not run counter
# 21
Why can an uplifting purpose not justify inventing a hadith?|The warning concerns deliberately attributing a false statement or action to the Prophet. A desirable lesson does not make that attribution true. The benefits emphasize its serious religious and worldly consequences and require verification before a report is spread.|E:statement or action
# 22
How did the permission to write answer the objection raised to Abdullah ibn Amr?|The objection concerned recording speech uttered in anger as well as pleasure. Abdullah referred the matter to the Prophet, who directed him to write and affirmed that nothing came from his mouth except truth. The response addresses the reliability of the teaching he was recording.|T:nothing comes out of it except the truth
What role does writing have in this account of preservation?|Abdullah wrote to preserve what he heard, and the benefits identify writing as a principal means of preserving knowledge. This establishes the value of recording teaching; it does not mean that every later copy or every statement found in writing is automatically accurate.|B4:methods for preserving
# 23
How does the publisher reconcile this writing restriction with permission to write hadith?|It prefers an earlier restriction followed by later permission, citing the instruction to write for Abu Shah and the permission given to Abdullah ibn Amr. It also mentions other explanations, including avoiding hadith and Quran on the same scroll. The preferred reconciliation should be identified as the publisher's preference, not the only explanation ever proposed.|E:first probability
Why is Hammam's uncertainty about the word deliberately retained?|The transmitted account expressly says that he thought the word had been included. Preserving that qualification distinguishes what the narrator reports confidently from what he reports with uncertainty. It is an example of care in transmission, not permission to silently regularize a text.|T:I think he also said
# 24
How can someone other than the original fabricator share responsibility for a false report?|The explanation concerns a person who reports what they know or strongly judge to be false. Culpable circulation can therefore extend the false attribution beyond its originator. The point is not that every learner's uncertainty amounts to fabrication; it is knowingly or culpably passing falsehood on as Prophetic teaching.|E:highly likely to be false
When may a fabricated report be mentioned according to the supplied benefit?|It may be mentioned to warn against it. That purpose must remain clear: identifying a report as fabricated is different from presenting it as authentic to encourage an audience. The exception reinforces verification rather than weakening it.|B3:warn against it
# 25
What does learning and teaching the Quran include beyond pronunciation?|The explanation includes recitation, memorization, measured reading, understanding, and interpretation, together with acting on the knowledge. The benefits also mention meanings and rulings. Teaching thus involves responsibly conveying what one has learned, rather than treating fluent reading as the whole of Quranic education.|E:understanding, and interpretation
Why are learning and teaching praised together?|Learning equips a person with knowledge; teaching enables others to benefit from it. The source praises those who do not keep their learning entirely to themselves. The explanation also links both activities with practice, so conveying knowledge does not replace responding to it personally.|B2:do not keep the knowledge
# 26
How does the report encourage both proficiency and perseverance?|It gives the proficient reciter the distinction of being with the noble and obedient angel-scribes, while promising two rewards to the reader who continues despite difficulty. The explanation identifies rewards for recitation and struggle. Both descriptions encourage learning; neither makes avoidable mistakes a goal.|E:one reward for his recitation
Do two rewards mean that struggling is necessarily superior to proficiency?|The quoted benefit expressly rejects that conclusion. The proficient reciter has a special rank and reward; the number two should not be used to erase it. A struggling learner is encouraged to continue while improving, rather than deliberately remaining inaccurate.|B2:greater reward than the proficient
# 27
Why does the Prophet compare memorized Quran to tethered camels?|The comparison explains the need for continued attention after memorization. Just as a shepherd's care keeps the camels from wandering, regular recitation helps preserve what has been learned. A practical response is to review earlier passages alongside learning new ones, rather than assuming initial memorization is permanent.|E:pays attention
# 28
What separates praiseworthy aspiration from blameworthy envy?|The explanation distinguishes wishing for similar good from wishing that another person lose their blessing. The praised examples concern beneficial use: rightful spending, applying wisdom, teaching, and sustained Quran recitation. The desire is directed toward sharing in good, rather than resenting someone else's possession of it.|E:free from malice
Why are the gifts described through what their recipients do with them?|Wealth is praised here through rightful spending, wisdom through application and teaching, and Quranic knowledge through recitation. The entry includes the wisdom wording from Ibn Masud and the accompanying Quran wording from Ibn Umar. The shared lesson is beneficial use of Allah's gifts, rather than possession or reputation alone.|E:beneficial knowledge
# 29
Why did the Companions find the verse about inward accountability difficult?|They were accustomed to duties they could perform, such as prayer and fasting, but feared accountability even for thoughts passing through their hearts. They sought clarification. The account then joins their concern with the Prophet's instruction, their willing response, and the assurance that Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity.|E:thoughts that pass through the heart
What does hearing and obeying mean in the course of this account?|It is a response of submission accompanied by seeking forgiveness and acknowledging the final return to Allah. The explanation describes both tongues and hearts responding. Their question did not prevent obedience; it led to clarification and a willing response rather than rejection of the command.|E:hearts submitted
How does the source distinguish passing thoughts from deliberate engagement?|The explanation excludes mere passing thoughts and whispers from the accountability they feared. The benefits distinguish these from dwelling on, being content with, or acting upon them. This qualification should be retained so that the account neither creates needless fear of involuntary thoughts nor removes responsibility for chosen conduct.|B8:passing thoughts
# 30
Who are the Quran's companions in the explanation of intercession?|The source describes those who recite it and act on its guidance. It particularly encourages al-Baqarah and Al Imran, the two radiant surahs, with recitation, reflection, and practice. The promise is presented through sustained engagement with the Quran, not merely owning a copy.|E:recite it and act upon it
What particular encouragement is given regarding al-Baqarah?|The narration contrasts blessing in taking recourse to it with grief in abandoning it and states that magicians cannot confront it. The explanation connects engagement with reciting, reflecting, and acting. These stated virtues should be taught without adding unrelated guarantees that the report does not give.|T:cause of grief
# 31
Why can the same Book be associated with elevation for some and lowering for others?|The explanation locates the difference in people's response. Those elevated believe its reports, observe its commands, and adopt its conduct. Others may recite well yet arrogantly reject its guidance. The lesson concerns how a person receives and follows the Book, rather than fluency or possession alone.|E:recite it well
# 32
Why is the exchange about Ayat al-Kursi more than a test of a verse's name?|Ubayy first defers, then identifies the verse beginning with Allah's oneness and the attributes Ever-Living and All-Sustaining. The Prophet affirms his answer and encourages his knowledge. The published benefit directs learners to memorize the verse, reflect on its meaning, and act accordingly.|B2:ponder its meanings
# 33
Where does the source place the boundary between sustained worship and excess?|It recommends fulfilling duties within one's ability while avoiding both neglect and burdens that lead to abandoning deeds. The benefits give the example of excessive voluntary night prayer causing someone to miss the obligatory Fajr prayer. A greater quantity of voluntary activity is not beneficial when it undermines a more important duty.|B6:sleeps
Why are active times and part of the night mentioned?|The explanation develops the image of a traveler who needs a sustainable pace. Suitable periods of energy help a person continue, whereas uninterrupted exertion can exhaust them. The qualification part of the night supports this point; it does not prescribe wakefulness throughout every night.|E:work at night is harder
How can using a lawful concession support devotion?|The benefits discuss a prescribed concession, such as tayammum when water cannot be used, as proper obedience rather than failure. Refusing the concession merely to choose a harder course can be excess. Its use remains tied to the circumstances and conditions for which it is prescribed.|B5:Tayammum
# 34
What makes the choice of ease principled rather than a search for convenience at any cost?|Aishah states the limit expressly: the easier option must not be sinful. If it involved sin, the Prophet kept far from it. Ease is therefore a preference within obedience, not a rule that makes any convenient choice acceptable.|T:unless it were a sin
How does the account distinguish personal forbearance from concern for Allah's limits?|He did not seek revenge merely for himself, yet the report distinguishes violations of Allah's sanctities. The explanation describes forgiveness of personal injury alongside adherence to divine limits. The distinction does not turn every private grievance or angry reaction into enforcement of Allah's rights.|E:own rights
# 35
Why is leaving a deed not necessarily evidence that the Prophet disliked it?|Aishah explicitly says that he might like a deed yet leave it out of concern that people would follow him and it could become obligatory for them. The reason for omission therefore matters. This account highlights compassion for the community rather than rejection of worship.|T:even though he liked
How does the explanation qualify Aishah's statement about Duha prayer?|It reads the apparent negation as lack of regular performance, not denial of every occurrence. The explanation says he sometimes offered it and sometimes left it for the concern already described. Aishah's own statement that she offered it should also be read as part of the account.|E:lack of regular performance
# 36
How does reward for guiding others relate to their own reward?|The caller receives a reward like that of those who follow, without diminishing theirs. The warning about misguidance follows the same structure: the influence can bring corresponding guilt without removing the followers' responsibility. The report treats moral influence as consequential rather than transferring all responsibility to one person.|T:without diminishing anything from their rewards
Can conduct influence others without an explicit invitation?|Yes. The explanation includes words and deeds, and the final benefit warns about public wrongdoing that others imitate even without being urged. The lesson applies to the example a person sets as well as what they formally teach.|B4:even if he does not urge
# 37
Why must the saying about a good precedent be read within the charity account?|People in poverty prompted an appeal, and one donor's example encouraged further giving. The explanation identifies a good practice here as already established in the religion. It concerns bringing a known good into action, not making an unsupported ritual valid simply because others copy it.|E:already established and rooted
What does encouraging even half a date contribute to the lesson?|The appeal includes different kinds and amounts of giving. Mentioning a small gift allows people of modest means to participate, while the substantial donation also becomes an example that encourages others. The lesson concerns responding to need according to one's means, not requiring one fixed amount from everyone.|T:half a date
# 38
How does the example of the Hajj pebbles explain the warning against excess?|The Prophet showed the small pebbles and instructed people to use their like, then warned against exceeding religious limits. The practical setting makes the principle concrete: greater size or severity is not automatically greater devotion. Following the demonstrated guidance is the relevant standard.|T:Throw the like of these
# 39
How are relieving hardship and helping others related to recompense?|The report promises corresponding divine relief for easing a believer's difficulty and states that Allah helps a person who helps their brother. The explanation includes practical assistance, money, and supplication. Service is therefore broader than a single form of charity, while the promised relief should not be replaced with an invented fixed worldly payment.|E:supplication, physical action, money
When can concealing a fault become protection of wrongdoing?|The benefits distinguish a private slip from persistence in corruption and harm. Concealment must not cancel correcting evil; the source discusses referring persistent wrongdoing to those in authority while considering the risk of greater harm. The instruction should not be used as a blanket reason to hide ongoing harm.|B5:persistence in transgression
What joins the promise about seeking knowledge with the blessing of Quran study gatherings?|The report encourages a path of learning and describes gathering to recite and study the Quran. The explanation connects sincere pursuit of religious knowledge with a path to Paradise, and names tranquillity, mercy, angels, and Allah's mention among the gathering's blessings. These are reasons for serious study, not substitutes for learning accurately.|E:for the sake of Allah
Why does the narration end by mentioning lineage?|It warns that ancestry cannot make up for deficient deeds. The explanation specifically cautions against relying on noble forefathers while neglecting one's own conduct. This closing point brings the preceding encouragements back to personal action: helping, learning, and responding to guidance.|T:lineage
# 40
How does the response to a request for curses express the Prophet's mission?|Instead of accepting cursing as his defining task, he states that he was sent as a mercy. The explanation describes a source of goodness for people and explains cursing as invoking exclusion from Allah's mercy. The response makes merciful purpose central to how the request is answered.|E:exclusion and expulsion
What change in everyday speech follows from this example?|The benefits connect following the Prophet with guarding the tongue against swearing and cursing and showing mercy to people. Religious commitment should therefore be expressed through disciplined, constructive speech rather than eagerness to insult or invoke harm in a disagreement.|B2:tongue pure
'''

def load_qa():
 out=[];group=[]
 for line in BLOCKS.strip().splitlines():
  if line.startswith('#'):
   if group:out.append(group)
   group=[];continue
  if not line.strip():continue
  q,a,basis=line.split('|');kind,anchor=basis.split(':',1)
  group.append({'q':q,'a':a,'basis':kind,'anchor':anchor})
 if group:out.append(group)
 assert len(out)==40 and all(1<=len(x)<=4 for x in out)
 return out
from expanded_learning import append_qa
FOCUSED_QA=append_qa(load_qa())
